---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Bench Vises in 2026: Tested and Ranked";
const description =
  "We clamped, hammered and levered against seven bench vises. Cast iron versus ductile iron, jaw width, throat depth, and which survive being hit.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "wilton-1755",
    name: "Wilton 1755 Tradesman",
    category: "5.5 in Jaw, Ductile Iron, 360 Swivel, 5.25 in Throat",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 1200,
    pros: [
      "Ductile iron construction that bends rather than shattering under abuse",
      "Very little jaw deflection under heavy clamping load",
      "Deep throat allowing large workpieces to sit well down in the jaws",
      "Smooth 360 degree swivel base that locks solidly",
      "Replaceable jaw inserts and pipe jaws built in",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive",
      "Very heavy, requiring a substantial bench",
      "More vise than light workshop use requires",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The vise you buy once. Ductile iron, minimal deflection, and it will outlive the bench it is bolted to.",
    description:
      "The failure mode that ends most bench vises is not wear, it is a crack. Grey cast iron is strong in compression and brittle under impact, and a vise that is hammered on, levered against, or dropped can shatter without warning. Ductile iron has graphite in nodular rather than flake form, which makes it far tougher: it deforms rather than fracturing. The Wilton Tradesman is built from it, and in our abuse testing it took hammer blows to the jaws and anvil that would have concerned us on a grey iron vise. Beyond durability, the measurable difference is deflection. Under heavy clamping load the jaws stayed close to parallel where cheaper vises visibly splayed at the top, which matters because splayed jaws grip only along one edge and let work rotate. The deep throat means a large workpiece can sit well down between the jaws rather than perched near the top, which is the difference between clamping something and merely touching it. The swivel base locks solidly rather than creeping under side load. The objections are cost and mass. It is expensive and extremely heavy, which means a bench built for it rather than the other way round.",
  },
  {
    id: "yost-750-di",
    name: "Yost 750-DI",
    category: "5 in Jaw, Ductile Iron, 360 Swivel, Pipe Jaws",
    badge: "Best Value Ductile",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 3400,
    pros: [
      "Ductile iron at a considerably lower price than premium vises",
      "Good jaw rigidity with limited deflection under load",
      "Integral pipe jaws and a usable anvil surface",
      "Smooth swivel base with a positive lock",
      "Replaceable jaw faces",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Machining and finish are less refined than premium vises",
      "Swivel lock is not as rigid as the best",
      "Heavy for a five inch vise",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Ductile iron toughness for far less money. The finish is rougher and the metallurgy is what matters.",
    description:
      "The argument for ductile iron is straightforward: it survives the abuse a bench vise actually receives, where grey cast iron eventually cracks. What has historically made it hard to recommend to a home workshop is the price gap. Yost narrows that considerably, offering a genuinely ductile iron vise at a price closer to grey iron competitors, and for a workshop where the vise gets hammered on that is the important specification. Our testing bore it out: jaw deflection under heavy clamping was well controlled, and the casting shrugged off impact testing. The integral pipe jaws below the main jaws are genuinely useful for anyone doing plumbing work, and the anvil surface is flat and solid enough for light forming. The swivel base rotates smoothly and locks positively. What separates it from premium vises is finish rather than function. The machining is rougher, the paint is basic, and the handle and screw feel less refined in operation. The swivel lock, while adequate, allows a little more movement under heavy side load than the best vises do. None of that affects whether the vise does its job, and for most workshops this is the sensible purchase.",
  },
  {
    id: "irwin-226361",
    name: "Irwin 226361 Multi-Purpose Vise",
    category: "6.5 in Jaw, Cast Iron, Swivel Base, Wide Jaws",
    badge: "Best Wide Jaw",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 7200,
    pros: [
      "Very wide 6.5 inch jaws that grip long workpieces along their length",
      "Large flat anvil surface",
      "360 degree swivel base",
      "Good value for the jaw width offered",
      "Pipe jaws included",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Grey cast iron rather than ductile, so it is more brittle under impact",
      "More jaw deflection under heavy load than premium vises",
      "Heavy without the durability that usually accompanies weight",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The widest jaws here for the money. Grey iron means treating it gently on the anvil.",
    description:
      "Jaw width determines how much of a workpiece the vise actually grips, and for anything long or awkward that is more important than clamping force. A narrow jaw holding a long board grips a small area and lets everything else flex; a wide jaw distributes the grip and holds the piece rigid. The Irwin 226361 offers 6.5 inches, wider than almost anything at the price, and for general workshop use where workpieces vary that width is genuinely useful. The anvil surface is large and flat, the swivel base rotates a full circle and locks adequately, and pipe jaws are included. For a general purpose vise it covers a great deal of ground. The compromise is the material. This is grey cast iron rather than ductile, which means it is strong in normal use and brittle under impact. Heavy hammering on the anvil, levering against the jaws with a long bar, or dropping it are all things that can crack it, and a cracked vise is scrap rather than repairable. Jaw deflection under heavy clamping is also greater than the ductile vises, showing as visible splay at the top of the jaws. Used sensibly it is good value; used hard it will eventually fail.",
  },
  {
    id: "yost-455",
    name: "Yost 455 Combination Vise",
    category: "5.5 in Jaw, Cast Iron, Pipe Jaws, Swivel",
    badge: "Best Combination",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 4900,
    pros: [
      "Well integrated pipe jaws that grip round stock securely",
      "Reasonable price for the size",
      "Adequate rigidity for general workshop use",
      "Swivel base with a positive lock",
      "Replaceable jaw faces",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Grey cast iron, so impact resistance is limited",
      "Screw action becomes stiff without periodic greasing",
      "Finish is basic",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A capable general purpose vise with proper pipe jaws. Fine for a home workshop that does not abuse it.",
    description:
      "Round stock is difficult to hold in flat jaws, because the contact area is a line rather than a face and any torque rotates the workpiece. Pipe jaws — a set of V-shaped serrated jaws below the main pair — grip round material properly, and for anyone working with pipe, tube or bar they turn an awkward job into a routine one. The Yost 455 integrates them well, with jaws that align properly and grip securely rather than being a token feature. As a general purpose vise it is competent. Rigidity is adequate for normal workshop use, the swivel base locks positively, and the jaw faces are replaceable when they eventually mark or wear. The price is reasonable for the size. The material is the usual caveat. Grey cast iron handles clamping loads well and impact loads poorly, so heavy hammering on the anvil or levering against the jaws is a risk rather than routine use. The screw action stiffens noticeably if it is not greased periodically, which is a five minute annual job that most owners never do and then blame the vise for. For a home workshop doing mixed work without abusing the tool, it is a sensible purchase.",
  },
  {
    id: "eclipse-mechanics-vise",
    name: "Eclipse Mechanics Vise 5 in",
    category: "5 in Jaw, Cast Steel, Fixed Base, Engineering Grade",
    badge: "Best for Metalwork",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 600,
    pros: [
      "Cast steel construction, tougher again than ductile iron",
      "Extremely rigid with minimal jaw deflection",
      "Precisely machined jaws that stay parallel under load",
      "Fixed base gives maximum rigidity",
      "Long standing engineering reputation",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Fixed base means no swivel, which limits versatility",
      "Expensive",
      "Heavy and less common than mainstream brands",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "An engineering vise rather than a general one. Nothing here holds metal more rigidly.",
    description:
      "There is a distinction between a general workshop vise and an engineering vise, and it comes down to precision under load. A metalworking vise must hold a part exactly where it was placed while it is filed, drilled or sawn, and any deflection or jaw splay shows up directly in the finished work. The Eclipse is built to that standard, with cast steel construction and jaws machined to stay parallel under clamping force. In our deflection testing it was the most rigid vise here by a clear margin. Cast steel is tougher than both grey and ductile iron, so impact resistance is excellent, and the precisely machined jaws grip a workpiece across their full face rather than along one edge. For filing flat, sawing square or drilling accurately, that rigidity is the difference between a good result and a struggle. The compromises are versatility and price. A fixed base gives maximum rigidity and no ability to rotate the work, which for general workshop use is a genuine limitation — swivelling a vise to bring a workpiece to a comfortable angle is something you do constantly. It is expensive and less widely distributed than mainstream brands. For a metalworking bench, it is the right tool.",
  },
  {
    id: "olympia-38-615",
    name: "Olympia Tools 38-615",
    category: "6 in Jaw, Cast Iron, Swivel Base, Budget",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.2,
    reviewCount: 6100,
    pros: [
      "Very low price for a six inch vise",
      "Adequate for light workshop and household use",
      "Swivel base included",
      "Pipe jaws and anvil surface present",
      "Widely available",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Noticeable jaw deflection under moderate load",
      "Grey cast iron with limited impact tolerance",
      "Screw and nut wear faster than better vises",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A lot of vise for very little money. Clamp things, do not hammer things.",
    description:
      "A workshop without a vise is missing something fundamental, and for a great many household tasks — holding a bolt while you cut it, gripping a bracket while you drill it, squeezing a bearing into place — almost any vise is transformative compared with none. The Olympia 38-615 provides six inch jaws, a swivel base, pipe jaws and an anvil surface at a price that requires no thought, which makes it a reasonable first vise. Where the price shows is in rigidity and durability. Jaw deflection under moderate clamping load was clearly visible, with the tops of the jaws splaying so that grip concentrated at the bottom edge. That reduces holding power and lets work rotate under torque. The grey cast iron construction has limited tolerance for impact, so the anvil surface should be treated as a flat area rather than a place to hammer. The screw and nut wear faster than better vises, and the action stiffens over time. None of that means it is a bad purchase at the price. It means it is a clamping tool rather than a working surface, and treating it that way will get years of use out of it.",
  },
  {
    id: "parkside-woodworking-vise",
    name: "Groz Woodworking Bench Vise 7 in",
    category: "7 in Jaw, Under Bench Mount, Quick Release, Wood Faces",
    badge: "Best Woodworking Vise",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 1300,
    pros: [
      "Mounts under the bench so the jaws sit flush with the benchtop",
      "Quick release mechanism for fast opening and closing",
      "Wide jaws designed to take wooden faces that will not mark work",
      "Deep capacity for holding boards on edge",
      "Proper woodworking geometry rather than an adapted metal vise",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires cutting into the bench to install properly",
      "Not suitable for metalwork or hammering",
      "Installation takes an afternoon",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A different tool entirely. If you work wood, a metalworking vise is the wrong answer and this is the right one.",
    description:
      "Metalworking vises and woodworking vises solve different problems and are not interchangeable, which catches out a lot of people who bolt a mechanics vise to a woodworking bench and wonder why it is awkward. A metal vise sits on top of the bench with narrow serrated jaws that mark timber and stand proud of the surface, obstructing work. A woodworking vise mounts underneath, with its jaws flush to the benchtop and faced with wood so nothing is marked. The Groz is a proper woodworking vise with the right geometry: wide jaws that grip a board across its width, deep capacity for holding a board on edge for planing or jointing, and a quick release that lets you open and close it in one motion rather than winding. Faced with hardwood jaw liners it holds work securely without marking it. For anyone planing, sawing or chiselling at a bench, this is the correct tool. The costs are installation and scope. It has to be mounted under the bench, which usually means cutting a recess and fitting a spacer — an afternoon of careful work. And it is not for metal, and definitely not for hammering.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Bench Vise",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-bench-vise/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best bench vise?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Wilton 1755 Tradesman for a lifetime tool, built from ductile iron that deforms rather than cracking under impact, with very little jaw deflection. Yost 750-DI offers the same ductile iron toughness for considerably less, and Eclipse is the pick for precision metalwork.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size bench vise do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Four to six inch jaw width covers almost everything a home workshop encounters. Also check throat depth, which determines how far down a workpiece can sit between the jaws, and maximum opening. Bigger is not automatically better, since a large vise dominates a small bench.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is ductile iron better than cast iron for a vise?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, meaningfully. Grey cast iron is strong in clamping and brittle under impact, so a sharp blow can crack the casting with no warning, and a cracked vise is scrap. Ductile iron deforms instead of fracturing, which matters because vises get hammered on.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How should I mount a bench vise?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Four through bolts with washers and nuts underneath, positioned directly over a bench leg rather than mid span, with the jaws flush with or slightly proud of the bench edge. Lag screws into the benchtop pull out over time as the wood compresses around them.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I stop a vise marking my work?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Fit soft jaws. Magnetic aluminium or plastic covers slip over the hardened serrated jaws and prevent marking entirely, and they cost very little. Two pieces of hardwood or thick leather do the same job for nothing and can be shaped for a particular workpiece.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use a metalworking vise for woodworking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Poorly. A metal vise sits on top of the bench with narrow serrated jaws that mark timber and stand proud of the surface, obstructing work. A woodworking vise mounts underneath with jaws flush to the top, faced with wood, and wide enough to grip a board properly.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-24",
      dateModified: "2026-08-24",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={`${title} | The Honest Reviewers`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-24",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-24",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools",
    tags: [
      "bench vise",
      "workshop vise",
      "ductile iron",
      "metalworking",
      "woodworking vise",
      "hand tools",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Bench Vises</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We clamped, hammered, levered and abused seven vises, measuring jaw deflection under load and checking whether the castings survive being hit. Only 7 earned a place on a bench.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#wilton-1755"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#cast-vs-ductile"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Cast Iron vs Ductile Iron
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 24, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#cast-vs-ductile" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Cast Iron vs Ductile</a>
          <a href="#specs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Sizing a Vise</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#mounting" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Mounting a Vise</a>
          <a href="#jaws" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Jaw Faces</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Types of Vise</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Vise Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A bench vise is the tool everything else in a workshop happens against, which means the important question is not how hard it clamps but whether it stays where you put it when you lean on the work. Jaw deflection under load, and whether the casting survives being struck, are the two things that separate a lifetime tool from a disappointment.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Cast Iron vs Ductile Iron vs Steel -->
        <div id="cast-vs-ductile" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Cast Iron vs Ductile Iron vs Steel
          </h2>

          <p>
            The material a vise is cast from determines how it fails, and vises fail by cracking rather than wearing out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Grey Cast Iron Is Strong and Brittle</h3>

          <p>
            Most affordable vises are grey iron, which handles clamping loads well and has graphite in flake form that acts as internal crack starters under impact. A sharp blow can propagate a crack across the casting with no warning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ductile Iron Bends Instead</h3>

          <p>
            Treating the iron so the graphite forms nodules rather than flakes makes it far tougher. Under an impact that would crack grey iron, ductile iron deforms. For a tool that gets hammered on, that is the difference that matters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cast Steel Is Tougher Again</h3>

          <p>
            Engineering vises in cast steel offer the highest toughness and rigidity, at correspondingly higher cost and weight. This is the specification for a metalworking bench where precision under load matters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Cracked Vise Is Scrap</h3>

          <p>
            Cast iron cannot be reliably welded in a home workshop, so a cracked body ends the tool. That is why material choice is really a question of how long you expect the vise to last rather than how well it works today.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight Alone Does Not Tell You</h3>

          <p>
            A heavy grey iron vise and a lighter ductile one can weigh similarly and behave completely differently under impact. If the listing does not state the material, assume grey iron.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Vise Specifications</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4-6 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Jaw width covering most workshops</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-5 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Throat depth worth having</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Ductile</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Material that survives being hit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">4</p>
