        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Jaw Width, Throat Depth and Opening
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three dimensions describe a vise and only one of them appears in the product name, which leads people to buy the wrong size.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Jaw width is the headline number.</strong> It is how much of the workpiece the jaws grip across, and wider grips longer work more securely. Four to six inches covers almost everything a home workshop encounters.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Throat depth decides what fits.</strong> This is how far down between the jaws a workpiece can sit before hitting the body. A shallow throat means large work is gripped near the top of the jaws where leverage works against you.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Maximum opening limits thickness.</strong> Usually similar to the jaw width, and it is worth checking against what you actually clamp. A vise that will not open wide enough for a piece of four by two is frustrating in a woodworking context.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bigger is not automatically better.</strong> A very large vise dominates a small bench, obstructs work, and its mass makes the bench harder to use for anything else. Match it to the bench and the work rather than buying the biggest available.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Swivel bases trade rigidity for versatility.</strong> Rotating a workpiece to a comfortable angle is genuinely useful, and the swivel joint is a flex point that a fixed base does not have. For general work the trade is worth it; for precision metalwork it is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the mounting footprint against your bench.</strong> Bolt spacing, base size and whether the bolts land on a solid part of the bench rather than an overhang all matter, and finding out after purchase is inconvenient.
          </p>
        </div>

