        <!-- SECTION 4: Mounting It Properly -->
        <div id="mounting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Mounting It Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            A vise transmits everything you do to the bench it is bolted to, and a poor installation undermines a good tool completely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use four bolts, not lag screws.</strong> Through bolts with washers and nuts underneath hold far better than lag screws driven into the bench top, which pull out over time as the wood compresses around them.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mount it at a bench leg.</strong> The corner of the bench directly above a leg is the most rigid point. A vise mounted in the middle of an unsupported span flexes the top and the whole bench moves when you lever against it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Set the jaw flush with the bench edge or slightly proud.</strong> Jaws set back from the edge prevent clamping long work vertically, because the workpiece hits the bench. Flush or a fraction proud is the correct position.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Add a spacer if the bench top is thin.</strong> A thin top under a heavy vise will crush and eventually split around the bolts. A hardwood spacer beneath spreads the load and stiffens the mounting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Height matters for comfort.</strong> For metalwork, the top of the jaws around elbow height suits filing and sawing. For woodworking, lower is better because planing uses body weight. A bench built for one is uncomfortable for the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Anchor the bench itself if it moves.</strong> A vise is only as rigid as what it is attached to, and a light bench that slides across the floor when you lever against the vise makes the whole installation useless. Bolt the bench to the wall or the floor.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Bolt Through, at a Leg</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Two decisions determine whether your vise feels solid: through bolts with nuts and washers rather than lag screws into the benchtop, and a position directly over a bench leg rather than mid span. Get both right and a modest vise feels rigid. Get either wrong and the best vise in this guide will feel like it is mounted on a trampoline.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Jaw Faces and Protecting Work -->
        <div id="jaws" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Jaw Faces and Protecting Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            The serrated hardened jaws that grip so well are also what puts diamond patterns into everything soft you clamp.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Serrated jaws mark almost everything.</strong> They are designed to bite, which is what stops work slipping under torque. On steel bar that is fine; on aluminium, brass, plastic, finished timber or a threaded part it is damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Soft jaws solve it cheaply.</strong> Magnetic aluminium or plastic covers slip over the hardened jaws in a second and prevent marking entirely. They cost very little and every workshop should own a set.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wood or leather faces work just as well.</strong> Two pieces of hardwood or thick leather held against the jaws do the same job for nothing, and are easy to shape for a particular workpiece.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Replaceable jaw faces are worth having.</strong> Serrations flatten over years and jaws get chewed by hardened parts. A vise with bolt-on faces can be renewed; one with integral jaws cannot.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>V-grooves hold round stock.</strong> Many vise jaws have a vertical or horizontal V machined into them for gripping bar and tube. Using them rather than the flats stops round work rotating.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never clamp so hard the work distorts.</strong> Thin tube, threaded fittings and machined parts crush easily, and a vise generates far more force than intuition suggests. Tighten until it holds, then stop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Mechanics, Woodworking and Specialist Vises -->
        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Mechanics, Woodworking and Specialist Vises
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bench vise covers several distinct tools, and buying the wrong category is a more expensive mistake than buying the wrong size.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A mechanics vise is the general purpose default.</strong> Bolted on top of the bench, with hardened serrated jaws, an anvil surface and usually pipe jaws underneath. It holds metal, plastic and awkward objects, and it is what most people mean by a bench vise.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A woodworking vise mounts underneath.</strong> The jaws sit flush with the benchtop and take wooden faces, so nothing is marked and the bench surface stays clear. Wide jaws grip a board across its width for planing, sawing and chiselling.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A machinist vise clamps to a machine table.</strong> Precision ground jaws, a low profile, and slots for bolting to a drill press or mill table. It is not a bench vise and using one as such wastes both its precision and your money.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Portable and clamp-on vises trade rigidity for flexibility.</strong> Useful on a workbench that has other jobs, or for taking to site. They flex under load and they are a compromise rather than an equivalent.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pipe vises grip round stock properly.</strong> A chain or yoke vise holds pipe securely for threading and cutting, which flat jaws cannot do without crushing it. Most mechanics vises include small pipe jaws that cover light work.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Most workshops want two.</strong> A mechanics vise at one corner of the bench and a woodworking vise at the other covers everything, and neither compromises the other. Trying to make one vise do both jobs is what produces frustration.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Looking After a Vise -->
        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Looking After a Vise
          </h2>

          <p>
            A bench vise is a simple machine and it will last a lifetime with about ten minutes of attention a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Grease the screw and nut annually.</strong> This is the single maintenance task that matters. A dry screw becomes stiff, wears the nut, and eventually strips. Wipe out the old grease, check for swarf, and apply fresh.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep swarf out of the slide.</strong> Metal chips work into the sliding jaw and act as an abrasive, wearing the ways. Brushing the vise off after metalwork prevents years of accumulated damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not use the handle as a lever.</strong> Slipping a pipe over the handle for extra force is how screws bend and nuts strip. Hand tight with the supplied handle is what the design assumes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Treat the anvil surface with respect.</strong> On grey iron vises it is a flat surface for light forming, not a real anvil. Heavy hammering is the most common cause of a cracked casting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never hammer on the sliding jaw.</strong> Force applied to the moving jaw is transmitted through the screw and the ways, neither of which is designed for impact. Support work against the fixed jaw when hammering.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the mounting bolts once a year.</strong> Repeated levering works bolts loose over time, and a vise that has developed a slight rock puts all of that movement into the bench top around the bolt holes. Half a turn on each nut annually keeps the installation as rigid as the day it went on.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Replace jaw faces before they are useless.</strong> Serrations flatten gradually, so grip declines slowly enough that you adapt without noticing until work starts slipping under load. Bolt-on faces are inexpensive and swapping them restores the vise completely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wipe it with oil in a damp workshop.</strong> Surface rust on the ways makes the action stiff and eventually pits them. A rag with light oil after use in a garage or unheated shop prevents it entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

