              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Four to six inch jaw width covers almost everything a home workshop encounters. Also check throat depth, which determines how far down a workpiece can sit between the jaws, and maximum opening. Bigger is not automatically better, since a large vise dominates a small bench.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Ductile or cast iron?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, meaningfully. Grey cast iron is strong in clamping and brittle under impact, so a sharp blow can crack the casting with no warning, and a cracked vise is scrap. Ductile iron deforms instead of fracturing, which matters because vises get hammered on.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I mount it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Four through bolts with washers and nuts underneath, positioned directly over a bench leg rather than mid span, with the jaws flush with or slightly proud of the bench edge. Lag screws into the benchtop pull out over time as the wood compresses around them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop it marking work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fit soft jaws. Magnetic aluminium or plastic covers slip over the hardened serrated jaws and prevent marking entirely, and they cost very little. Two pieces of hardwood or thick leather do the same job for nothing and can be shaped for a particular workpiece.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use one for woodwork?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Poorly. A metal vise sits on top of the bench with narrow serrated jaws that mark timber and stand proud of the surface, obstructing work. A woodworking vise mounts underneath with jaws flush to the top, faced with wood, and wide enough to grip a board properly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Bench Vise Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vises are simple, and the ways people ruin them are consistent enough to be worth listing.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Hammering on the Anvil Surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On a grey cast iron vise the anvil is a flat surface for light forming, not a real anvil, and heavy hammering is the most common cause of a cracked casting. A cracked vise cannot be reliably repaired and becomes scrap. If you need an anvil, buy an anvil.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Extending the Handle for More Force</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Slipping a pipe over the handle generates far more force than the screw and nut were designed for, which bends screws and strips nuts. A vise tightened by hand with the supplied handle already exerts more clamping force than most work can survive.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mounting With Lag Screws Mid Span</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lag screws driven into a benchtop pull out over time as the wood compresses, and a vise mounted between the legs flexes the whole bench when you lever against it. Four through bolts, over a bench leg, is what makes a vise feel solid.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Greasing the Screw</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ten minutes once a year is the entire maintenance requirement, and skipping it is why old vises feel stiff and eventually strip their nuts. Wipe the old grease off, check for metal chips that have worked into the thread, and apply fresh.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

